@jun23_protocol
title: Return with protocols, legal defence and named deadlines
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: No legal-medical operation is ready.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; health_policy_pressure += 4; feminist_trust += 5; public_trust += 3; progressive_credibility += 2; last_policy_stage = "Implementation proposal"; last_policy_authority = "Parliamentary opposition"; last_policy_implementation = 0; news_headline = "The Left returns with an implementation plan, not implemented policy"

The answer is administrative rather than symbolic: protocols, deadlines and a
named legal-medical operation. That gives supporters something more concrete
than grief, while reminding everyone that implementation still depends on power
the party does not yet hold.

- @jun23_return: Return to the June desk

@jun23_autonomy
title: Support autonomous local marches without party ownership
on-arrival: movement_autonomy += 6; feminist_trust += 4; organiser_energy -= 3; women_support += 3; left_poll_momentum += 0.1; news_headline = "Local marches keep their names and gain parliamentary support"

Lewica lends platforms, MPs and logistics without trying to rebrand the marches.
That protects trust with organisers and leaves the party less able to turn the
renewed anger into a visibly partisan campaign asset.

- @jun23_return: Return to the June desk

@jun23_bill
title: Put the repeal bill and Left branding at the front
on-arrival: progressive_credibility += 4; movement_autonomy -= 5; spring_strength += 2; razem_dissent += 1; feminist_trust += 1; news_headline = "A repeal bill competes with the movement's immediate demands"

The party gives supporters a banner, a bill number and a parliamentary demand.
It also reopens the old suspicion that movement grief is being translated too
quickly into branding that the organisers did not ask us to own.

- @jun23_return: Return to the June desk

@jun23_maternal_truce
title: Avoid repeal and lead with maternity wards, hospice care and family security
subtitle: A welfare-conservative answer builds Social Patriots while rights caucuses harden against the leadership.
on-arrival: health_policy_pressure += 4; household_policy_pressure += 3; social_patriot_support += 9; left_cultural_position += 5; left_right_score += 5; rural_support += 2; pensioner_support += 1; psl_relation += 4; feminist_trust -= 10; progressive_credibility -= 7; progressives_dissent += 12; spring_dissent += 9; razem_dissent += 9; party_unity -= 4; public_mood_pending_abortion_rights_support += -6; public_mood_pending_secular_state_support += -4; news_headline = "Lewica answers a death under the abortion ban with family services but no repeal"

The leadership offers a family-services answer to a rights crisis and makes the
internal break impossible to miss. Social patriots gain a clearer language;
the movement and the rights caucuses gain a sharper reason to distrust it.

- @jun23_return: Return to the June desk

@jun23_nowa_solidarnosc
# prose-audit: Grade A — The tiny press conference names the founders, programme and intended target before the punchline lands one week later.
title: A patriotic challenger appears
face-image: img/poland/events/nowasolidarnoscevent.jpg
new-page: true
view-if: nowa_solidarnosc_formed = 0
on-arrival: nowa_solidarnosc_formed = 1; news_headline = "Nowa Solidarność announces a challenge to the parliamentary Left"

= 15 June 2023 — A challenger appears

At a small Warsaw press conference, party chair **Chrystian Szpilski**,
secretary-general **Patryk Spaliński** and co-founder **Maciej Kozłowski**
present <span class="party party-nowa-solidarnosc">**Nowa Solidarność**</span>.
Publicly backed by urban activist **Jan Śpiewak**,
the new party promises a patriotic, economically left alternative, an
independent run for the Sejm and Senate, and a direct challenge to the
parliamentary Left.

Three leaders, one banner and one week of online attention: at last, we have a
challenger.

- @jun23_nowa_solidarnosc_dissolves: See how its first week goes


@jun23_nowa_solidarnosc_dissolves
# prose-audit: Grade A — The follow-up turns the documented resignations and vanishing accounts into a factual one-week dissolution gag.
title: The challenger dissolves
face-image: img/poland/events/nowasolidarnoscevent.jpg
new-page: true
on-arrival: news_headline = "Nowa Solidarność disappears after one week"

= 22 June 2023 — A challenger disappears

Spaliński resigns as secretary-general, withdraws from the party and apologises
for joining it. He disowns Szpilski over the chair's business connections and
past claim that Crimea is Russian. Kozłowski's account disappears; then so do
the accounts of Szpilski and Nowa Solidarność itself.

Whatever may remain in the party register, the functioning challenger is over.
<span class="party party-nowa-solidarnosc">**Nowa Solidarność**</span> has
achieved solidarity for seven days.

- @jun23_nowa_solidarnosc_closes: Return to the June desk

